## Known weak points

- The eye catch-light is 8 mm across on a 21 mm eye dot, so it covers about 38% of each eye. It
  reads as a cartoon catch-light at 1024 px but is large for a "small" light. The owner fixed the
  0.004 m radius.
- `forge inspect` warns that `eye-glint` is "barely visible (under 0.3% of the silhouette in every
  view)" at its 512 px views. It is not floating and not buried. The warning is a consequence of
  the fixed 0.004 m radius; a larger dot would pass the threshold.
- The wings are the kind's smooth paddles. At 1024 px they read as folded wings; at 128 px the
  pale grey-white edge `#dcd8cf` against `#f4f1ea` is subtle, so the wing shape may not read in the
  smallest sprite.
- The tail is a small upturned tuft (`tailPose` scale 0.7). It reads in the side and back views
  only faintly.
- "One body per material" is not literal: the kind puts white on `body` and `tail-fan`, orange on
  `legs`, `bill`, and `webs`. The owner accepted the split.
- The kind's `jaw` bone now has no geometry (the beak bodies are dropped). The jaw motion in the
  fly, attack, hit, and death clips is invisible. The skeleton and the clips are the kind's, as the
  brief asks, but the duck does not show a jaw.
- There is no duck mockup, so the asset has no reference image and nobody compared it against a
  drawn duck. The brief is the target.
- The head and neck were retuned late (neckScale 1.15, headLift 0.025) after the first look; the
  final review is from 512 and 1024 px stills, not from a moving clip.
- The plan task stays `[~]`; this role does not mark it `[x]`.

